### What is StringJoiner?

```java
import java.util.StringJoiner;

StringJoiner sj = new StringJoiner(", ", "[", "]");
sj.add("SAV2001");
sj.add("CUR3001");
sj.add("FD4001");
System.out.println(sj.toString());  // [SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001]

// Empty StringJoiner with setEmptyValue:
StringJoiner empty = new StringJoiner(", ", "[", "]");
empty.setEmptyValue("[no accounts]");
System.out.println(empty.toString());  // [no accounts]
```

### How do the three options relate?

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "FD4001");

// Option 1: StringJoiner (imperative)
StringJoiner sj = new StringJoiner(", ", "[", "]");
for (String id : accounts) sj.add(id);
String result1 = sj.toString();

// Option 2: String.join (static helper, no prefix/suffix)
String result2 = String.join(", ", accounts);

// Option 3: Collectors.joining (stream)
String result3 = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));

// All produce the same formatted string.
```

### What is the comparison table?

| Context | Use | Prefix/Suffix | Empty fallback |
|---|---|:---:|:---:|
| Imperative loop with conditions | StringJoiner | Yes | setEmptyValue |
| Simple delimiter join | String.join | No | N/A |
| Stream pipeline | Collectors.joining | Yes | N/A |

### How does StringJoiner handle conditional adds?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.StringJoiner;

public class ConditionalJoin {
    record Account(String id, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", true),
            new Account("SAV2002", false),
            new Account("CUR3001", true));

        // StringJoiner with condition (imperative)
        StringJoiner sj = new StringJoiner(", ", "Active: [", "]");
        for (Account a : accounts) {
            if (a.active()) sj.add(a.id());
        }
        System.out.println(sj.toString()); // Active: [SAV2001, CUR3001]

        // Stream equivalent
        String result = accounts.stream()
            .filter(Account::active)
            .map(Account::id)
            .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.joining(", ", "Active: [", "]"));
        System.out.println(result); // Active: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
    }
}
```

### How does merge work for parallel streams?

StringJoiner.merge(other) combines two joiners, which is how Collectors.joining handles parallel streams.

```java
StringJoiner sj1 = new StringJoiner(", ");
sj1.add("SAV2001").add("SAV2002");

StringJoiner sj2 = new StringJoiner(", ");
sj2.add("CUR3001").add("CUR3002");

sj1.merge(sj2);
System.out.println(sj1.toString()); // SAV2001, SAV2002, CUR3001, CUR3002
```

### How does setEmptyValue work?

```java
StringJoiner sj = new StringJoiner(", ", "[", "]");
sj.setEmptyValue("[no results]");

// Without any adds:
System.out.println(sj.toString()); // [no results]

// With adds:
sj.add("SAV2001");
System.out.println(sj.toString()); // [SAV2001]
```

Without setEmptyValue, an empty joiner returns just prefix+suffix: `[]`.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: using + concatenation in a loop
String result = "";
for (String id : accounts) {
    result += id + ", "; // O(n^2) string copying!
}
// Trailing comma, poor performance

// CORRECT: StringJoiner or String.join
String result = String.join(", ", accounts); // O(n), no trailing comma
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- StringJoiner.merge(other) combines two joiners, which is how Collectors.joining handles parallel streams.
- String.join uses StringJoiner internally: the implementation is `new StringJoiner(delimiter).add(elements)...toString()`.
- For conditional adds (only join non-empty strings), StringJoiner with an if-check is cleaner than stream+filter+joining.
- StringJoiner is NOT thread-safe. Use Collectors.joining for parallel streams, which handles merging internally.
- setEmptyValue replaces the default prefix+suffix output for empty joiners with a custom message.
